README file for Programming Assignment II (Parser - cool.y)
===========================================================

1. Overview
-----------
This assignment implements a parser for the COOL (Classroom Object-Oriented
Language) using GNU Bison. The parser receives a stream of tokens produced by the
Cool lexical analyzer, constructs an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) rooted at a
`Program` node using semantic actions and constructor functions provided by the
Cool tree package (`cool-tree.h`), and performs robust error recovery.

2. Design & Implementation Details
----------------------------------

a. Grammar Structure & AST Construction:
   - Program & Classes:
     The root of the AST is constructed by `program(class_list)`.
     Classes default to inheriting from `Object` when no explicit `inherits`
     clause is provided. Class nodes are created via:
     `class_(name, parent, features, filename)`
   - Features:
     Methods are defined by `method(name, formals, return_type, expr)` and
     attributes by `attr(name, type_decl, init_expr)`. Attributes without an
     explicit initialization expression default to `no_expr()`.
   - Lists:
     All AST lists (`Classes`, `Features`, `Formals`, `Expressions`, `Cases`)
     are constructed using `nil_<Type>()`, `single_<Type>()`, and
     `append_<Type>()` functions.
   - Expressions:
     Every expression form defined in Figure 1 of the Cool Reference Manual is
     supported:
     * Assignment: `assign(id, expr)`
     * Dynamic dispatch: `dispatch(expr, method_id, actuals)`
     * Static dispatch: `static_dispatch(expr, type_id, method_id, actuals)`
     * Self dispatch shorthand `f(...)`: syntactic sugar for `self.f(...)`
     * Conditionals: `cond(pred, then_exp, else_exp)`
     * While loops: `loop(pred, body)`
     * Block expressions: `block(expressions)`
     * Let bindings: nested `let(id, type, init, body)` nodes
     * Case expressions: `typcase(expr, cases)` with branch nodes `branch(...)`
     * Constructors & Tests: `new_(type)`, `isvoid(expr)`
     * Arithmetic & Logic: `plus`, `sub`, `mul`, `divide`, `neg` (~),
       `lt` (<), `leq` (<=), `eq` (=), `comp` (not)
     * Constants & Objects: `object`, `int_const`, `string_const`, `bool_const`

b. Operator Precedence & Resolving the "Let-In" Ambiguity:
   - Operators are assigned precedence levels from lowest to highest:
     * `LETPREC` (dummy token with lowest precedence)
     * `ASSIGN` (right-associative)
     * `NOT` (non-associative)
     * `LE`, `<`, `=` (non-associative)
     * `+`, `-` (left-associative)
     * `*`, `/` (left-associative)
     * `ISVOID` (non-associative)
     * `~` (non-associative)
     * `@` (non-associative)
     * `.` (non-associative)
   - The Cool `let` construct extends as far to the right as possible. To resolve
     shift/reduce conflicts, `let_expr` productions ending with `IN expr` are
     associated with the low-precedence pseudo-token `%prec LETPREC`. As a result,
     subsequent operators shift onto the inner expression rather than prematurely
     reducing the outer let expression.

c. Error Handling & Recovery:
   Using Bison's `error` token and synchronization tokens, the parser recovers in
   all 4 required scenarios:
   1. Class definitions:
      `class_list error ';'` catches errors in class definitions up to the class
      terminating `;`, allowing parsing to restart cleanly at the next class.
   2. Features within a class:
      `feature_list error ';'` synchronizes on `;`, discarding the malformed
      feature and continuing to the next feature.
   3. Let variable bindings:
      `error ',' let_expr` synchronizes on `,`, discarding the faulty variable
      declaration and continuing with the remaining bindings in the let list.
   4. Expressions in a `{ ... }` block:
      `expr_block error ';'` synchronizes on `;`, discarding the faulty expression
      and continuing with subsequent expressions in the block.

3. Testing Strategy (`good.cl` & `bad.cl`)
-----------------------------------------
- `good.cl`:
  Exercises every single legal construct in Cool: classes with and without
  inheritance, methods with various parameter counts, attributes with and without
  initialization, self/object/static dispatch, nested loops and conditionals,
  chained let bindings, case expressions, all arithmetic, boolean, and comparison
  operators.
- `bad.cl`:
  Specifically exercises the 4 error recovery requirements:
  1. Errors in class definitions followed by valid classes.
  2. Errors in attributes and methods followed by valid features.
  3. Errors in individual let bindings recovering at commas.
  4. Errors in expressions within a `{ ... }` block recovering at semicolons.

